[]($section.id('intro'))
Hi, I'm **Rene Schallner**. I build software, tools and products where AI meets
research. I run [AI Research & Technology Lab](https://technologylab.ai) in
Vienna, where I'm building [QualCode](https://qualcode.ai) and
[LaBrowser](https://labrowser.technologylab.ai). Before that: Chief Founding
Engineer at [ZML](https://zml.ai), eight years at [NIM](https://nim.org), medical
tech and telecom.

Most of my side projects start with a question: **can I do _this_ without
_that_?** I build the thing, use it for real, and write down the verdict. The
good ones grow up and get a successor. [More about me](/about/).

[]($section.id('tree'))
My projects aren't a list. They're a family tree: each line is a lineage, and
the good ideas keep coming back in the next project.

[]($section.id('lineages'))
- **Notes:** [sublime_zk](/projects/sublime_zk/) → [sublimeless_zk](/projects/sublimeless_zk/) → [telekasten.nvim](/projects/telekasten/) → [omajop](https://github.com/renerocksai/omajop) → [omajot](https://github.com/renerocksai/omajot). Zettelkasten tools since 2017, one editor at a time.
- **Web, in Zig:** [zap](/projects/zap/) → [bounded/http](https://github.com/technologylab-ai/bounded-http) → [baz](https://github.com/technologylab-ai/baz). From a C foundation to pure Zig with hard limits.
- **Slides:** [Bûllets](/projects/bullets/) → [slides](/projects/slides/) → [rayslides](https://github.com/technologylab-ai/rayslides). Plain text in, great talk out. Third attempt, best one.
- **No cloud:** [dockercr](https://github.com/renerocksai/dockercr) → [omajot](https://github.com/renerocksai/omajot). If SSH or Tailscale can do the job, I don't need somebody's cloud.

**omajot is where the lines meet:** notes from the green line, a hub served by
baz from the orange one, and the no-cloud rule from the blue one.

[]($section.id('omarchy'))
[Omarchy](https://omarchy.org) is my desktop. When I miss something in the bar,
I write a plugin for it. There are four so far, and each one installs with a
single command.

[]($section.id('plugins'))
># [omajot]($block.attrs('plugin'))
>
> [](<$image.siteAsset('profile/omarchy/omajot.webp').alt('The omajot main window on Omarchy')>)
>
>My own notes app, synced by my own hub. A dropdown and a main window in the
>bar, full keyboard control, and the same notes in the terminal and on my
>phone. [Repo →](https://github.com/renerocksai/omajot)

># [omajop]($block.attrs('plugin'))
>
> [](<$image.siteAsset('profile/omarchy/omajop.webp').alt('The omajop panel: folders and tags, notes, and the rendered note')>)
>
>A Joplin notes browser: folders and tags, the note list and the rendered
>note, one click away. omajot's predecessor. When you're ready to move, omajot
>imports Joplin in one command. [Repo →](https://github.com/renerocksai/omajop)

># [omapress]($block.attrs('plugin'))
>
> [](<$image.siteAsset('profile/omarchy/omapress.webp').alt('The omapress panel listing the latest Omarchy news')>)
>
>Omarchy news in the bar. The newspaper turns red when there are unread posts,
>and a keyboard-driven panel reads them without a browser. Works offline.
>[Repo →](https://github.com/renerocksai/omapress)

># [neomarchy]($block.attrs('plugin'))
>
> [](<$image.siteAsset('profile/omarchy/neomarchy.webp').alt('The neomarchy panel with favorite neowake sessions and a player')>)
>
>Search, favorite and play [neowake](https://app.neowake.de/) sessions from the
>bar. Playback runs through mpv with MPRIS, so the media keys just work.
>[Repo →](https://github.com/renerocksai/neomarchy)

[]($section.id('install'))
```sh
omarchy plugin add https://github.com/renerocksai/omajot --enable
omarchy plugin add https://github.com/renerocksai/omajop --enable
omarchy plugin add https://github.com/renerocksai/omapress --enable
omarchy plugin add https://github.com/renerocksai/neomarchy --enable
```

omajot also needs its hub on an always-on machine: see
[Get started](https://renerocks.ai/omajot/get-started.html).

[]($section.id('cases'))
The question, the trade, the verdict.

># [Synced notes with no cloud at all?]($block.attrs('case'))
>[omajot](https://github.com/renerocksai/omajot) · Zig · CRDT · WASM · Tailscale
>
>Apple-Notes-like markdown notes in the Omarchy bar, in the terminal, and on my
>iPhone. No Dropbox: a small Zig hub syncs every device over Tailscale. The hub
>runs on my always-on M3 Max, the same box my coding agents already live on.
>Hub-and-spoke is a pattern I trust, so notes got the same treatment. A CRDT
>written in Zig merges concurrent edits, and the same Zig core runs natively on
>the desktop and as `core.wasm` in the phone's web app. The hub is a
>[baz](https://github.com/technologylab-ai/baz) app.
>
>**Trade:** Dropbox's zero-setup sync, for notes that never leave my own
>machines, work offline, never conflict, and that agents can script.
>
>[✓ verdict]($text.attrs('verdict')) **Daily driver.** My notes live there now.

># [A container registry with nothing but SSH?]($block.attrs('case'))
>[dockercr](https://github.com/renerocksai/dockercr) · Docker · SSH
>
>omajot's older sibling in spirit. ghcr.io has download limits. A self-hosted
>registry wants TLS, a CA and access tokens. dockercr skips all of it: the
>registry only listens on loopback, and SSH port forwarding does the
>authentication and the encryption. No Docker Hub, no certificates.
>
>[✓ verdict]($text.attrs('verdict')) Push to production like a boss.

># [Fast, and never past its limits?]($block.attrs('case'))
>[bounded/http](https://github.com/technologylab-ai/bounded-http) + [baz](https://github.com/technologylab-ai/baz) · Zig 0.16 · io_uring · kqueue · IOCP
>
>[Zap](/projects/zap/) made Zig backends blazingly fast on top of facil.io, a C
>library. bounded/http replaces that foundation with pure Zig on `io_uring`,
>`kqueue` and IOCP, inspired by [TigerStyle](https://tigerstyle.dev):
>connections, buffers, queues and workers are reserved up front. When capacity
>runs out, it pushes back instead of growing. On Linux it needs no libc, so a
>server can be one static binary. Baz (*Bounded Async Zap*) puts Zap's typed
>endpoints on top, with streaming, SSE, Mustache templates and cookies.
>
>[✓ verdict]($text.attrs('verdict')) Good enough to trust with my notes: it runs the omajot hub.

># [Can I build the CPU I drew in the nineties?]($block.attrs('case'))
>[rrisc](/projects/rrisc/) · VHDL · FPGA
>
>In the early nineties I designed a small *Radical RISC* CPU on paper. At
>Christmas 2020 I finally built it: VHDL, simulated with ghdl and gtkwave from
>vim and tmux, with its own assembler, running on a Xilinx Spartan-7 FPGA board.
>[The whole story](https://renerocksai.github.io/rrisc) is written up, from
>microcode sketches to the first executed instruction.
>
>[✓ verdict]($text.attrs('verdict')) Nearly thirty years late, and it runs.

># [Can slides stay plain text and still not suck?]($block.attrs('case'))
>[rayslides](https://github.com/technologylab-ai/rayslides) · Zig · raylib
>
>My third answer to the same question. [Bûllets](/projects/bullets/) (2020)
>turned the Godot game engine into a slide show: interactive animations, a game
>in your deck, and a multiplayer mode. [Slides](/projects/slides/) (2021, my
>first Zig project) went small: one executable, Dear ImGui and OpenGL, a
>markdown-ish text format, at 10 000 FPS. rayslides, built on raylib, keeps the
>readable source and adds a visual Studio that stays in sync with it, reveals,
>transitions and morph animations, and an embedded Neovim. Bûllets' multiplayer
>grew up, too: it's now a phone remote with speaker notes, plus Crowdplay
>audience polls.
>
>[✓ verdict]($text.attrs('verdict')) Nobody has noticed I'm not using PowerPoint.

># [Can a database built for money sell conference tickets?]($block.attrs('case'))
>[TigerFans](/projects/tigerfans/) · TigerBeetle · FastAPI · Python
>
>A ticket booking system on [TigerBeetle](https://tigerbeetle.com). Tickets and
>goodies are accounts and transfers. A checkout places a time-limited hold, a
>pending transfer, and the payment webhook either finalizes or voids it. It
>uses FastAPI, Redis and PostgreSQL, has load tests, and I wrote an
>auto-batcher for the async TigerBeetle client along the way. The
>[write-up](/blog/2025-11-02--tigerfans/) has the details, or
>[try it live](https://tigerfans.io).
>
>[✓ verdict]($text.attrs('verdict')) If you can reserve it or run out of it, TigerBeetle can count it.
